WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:16.000
[SQUEAKING] [RUSTLING] [CLICKING]

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:22.000
RAMA RAMAKRISHNAN: So let's start with a quick review. Last week, we looked at BERT, how BERT was created.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:27.000
And we learned about this technique called masking, which is a kind of self-supervised learning.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:32.000
And the idea of masking was very simple. We asked ourselves the question. We have seen ways in which people

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:38.000
can take images and pre-trained models like ResNet on vast body of images.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:43.000
But then for each image, somebody had to go and label them. So for text, we asked the question, well,

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:51.000
what does it mean to label a piece of text when we don't actually have a clearly defined end goal in mind, except the general goal of pre-training things?

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:57.000
And then we said oh, well, what we can do is we can actually replace some of the words in every sentence

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:02.000
with what we call a mask token. And then we just train the network to recover the blanks,

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:09.000
to fill in the blanks. And this technique, which is one of many ways of doing what's called self-supervised learning, is called masking.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:15.000
And we described how, if you essentially take all of Wikipedia, and for every sentence,

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:21.000
you mask it like this, and then train a network to recover, to fill in the blanks, the resulting network

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:26.000
becomes really good at doing all kinds of interesting things. And that, in fact, the first such network,

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:32.000
or one of the first such networks, was called BERT. And in fact in your homework, you've been looking at BERT and so on and so forth.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:38.000
That's masking. Now we're going to switch gears and talk about a different kind of self-supervised learning, which

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:45.000
is different from masking, which turns out to be weirdly more interesting and powerful.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:50.000
So we are going to look at another technique. And this technique is called next word prediction.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:55.000
So now it is actually, in some sense, a special case of masking, where you're basically saying,

00:01:55.000 --> 00:02:01.000
take a sentence and, instead of randomly picking a word and making it a blank, you're saying,

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:07.000
I'm just going to take the last word and make it a blank. And then you send the sentence in.

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:14.000
And then you have the machine just fill in the blank on the last word, predict the next word. And you don't have to use full sentences for it.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:20.000
You can use parts of sentences for it, sentence fragments as well. So if you take the same sentence as before,

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:26.000
the mission of the MIT Sloan School, you can literally divide it into-- well, you can give the and ask it to predict mission.

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:31.000
If you can give it the mission and ask it to predict of. You give it the mission, of, ask to predict the.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:36.000
You get the idea. So every sentence fragment, you can take, and literally just give it the first few and then predict

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:42.000
the next one, first few, next one, first few, next one. So this is next word prediction.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:49.000
And so what we're going to do now is we're going to actually take the transformer encoder

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:55.000
architecture that we used to build BERT in the last class. And we're going to try to use it to solve next word prediction,

00:02:55.000 --> 00:03:01.000
to build a model that can do next word prediction. So this is what we have.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:10.000
So what we're going to do is, if you take the phrase, the cat sat on the mat, so the phrase was, let's say,

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:20.000
the cat sat on the mat, so what you might want to do is to say,

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:25.000
OK, this is the input.

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:33.000
Output, the cat. Then maybe you have the cat.

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:39.000
And then the output is sat. The cat sat on, and so on.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:45.000
You get the idea. And then, finally, we have the cat sat, whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa,

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.000
the mat. This, basically, what we have, all these inputs and outputs. But we're going to very compactly express it

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:58.000
as if we're just coming in through as one sort of data point in one batch. And that's what we're doing here.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:04.000
So what we're going to do is we're going to stack it up like this, where we have the cat sat on the, on the left,

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:11.000
meaning everything but the last word. And then we're going to take that same sentence and just shift it to the left one.

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:19.000
So the cat sat on the mat, we cut off the mat, right? And that becomes the input. Then we cut off the first word, and that becomes the output.

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:27.000
So when you look at it that way, you can see here, the, you will want the to be used to predict cat,

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:33.000
you will want the cat to be used to predict sat, and so on and so forth.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:41.000
So this is just a little sort of manipulation so that we don't have to have dozens of sentences or sentence

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:47.000
examples just for one starting sentence. So if you have something like this, what you can do

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:52.000
is you can run it through positional input embeddings, like we have done before with BERT.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:58.000
Then we can run it through a whole bunch of transformers. It's like a transformer stack.

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:03.000
Then we get these contextual embeddings. Then we run them through maybe one or more ReLUs, if you want,

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:11.000
because it's always a good idea to stick some ReLUs at the very end. And then we basically attach a softmax

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:17.000
to every one of the things that are coming out. And then that softmax is actually

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:24.000
going to be a softmax whose range is the entire vocabulary.

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:30.000
For now, let's assume that the vocabulary is just a vocabulary of words, not tokens. We'll get into tokens a bit later on in the class.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:37.000
For now, just assume it's words. And roughly speaking, let's say there are 50,000 words in our vocabulary. So each of these softmaxes-- and this

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:45.000
is exactly what we did for BERT, by the way. Each of these softmaxes is like a 50,000 way softmax.

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:51.000
But what we're going to do is, here, when we look at it this way, since we are fundamentally

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:58.000
bothered about next word prediction, as you will see later on, we are actually going to ignore all these predictions because who cares?

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:03.000
We are only going to look at the last one to figure out, OK, what is the last prediction?

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:09.000
What is it? Because the last prediction is going to be based on everything that came before it here.

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:16.000
So this is really the next word that's actually being predicted. All the things before, we don't care so much.

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:24.000
And all this will become slightly clearer because we're going to make a couple of passes through it. Yeah? AUDIENCE: How do we predict the end of a sentence then?

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:30.000
RAMA RAMAKRISHNAN: So the notion of a sentence has disappeared at this point. What we're going to do is, when we

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:36.000
look at how we tokenize the input for these kinds of models, we're actually going to take punctuation into account.

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:41.000
AUDIENCE: Oh. RAMA RAMAKRISHNAN: So we're going to take periods into account, exclamation marks into account, and so on and so forth. And that will answer your question,

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:49.000
and we'll come back to that. So this is what we have. So all right, so just to be clear,

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:54.000
the embedding that's coming out of the final dense layer is passed through its own softmax with the number of softmax categories

00:06:54.000 --> 00:07:03.000
equal to the vocab size. All right, so first of all, so let's

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:09.000
say we train models, a model like this, with lots of inputs and outputs. This just looks like BERT.

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:15.000
It's not that different, except that there is no notion of a mask. Do you notice any problems with the way

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:21.000
this thing has been set up? AUDIENCE: For some words, like the,

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:26.000
you're going to have a lot of potential output pairs that come out of that. RAMA RAMAKRISHNAN: True, which means

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:32.000
that if you have a word like the, the next word-- AUDIENCE: Very hard to predict. RAMA RAMAKRISHNAN: True. So some words may be hard to predict,

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:38.000
depending on the last word of the sentence that was the input. That's what you're getting at, yeah.

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:43.000
Other concerns? So I want you-- yeah?

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:50.000
AUDIENCE: Since you're using contextual on this, the output of the first word is going to have access

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:55.000
to the second word. And so it's kind of like cheating. RAMA RAMAKRISHNAN: Bingo.

00:07:55.000 --> 00:08:02.000
So remember, bingo is a technical term in deep learning, which means great.

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:07.000
So if you go to this, as she points out,

00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:15.000
if you look at the self-attention layer, note, remember, the self-attention layer is the key building block of the transformer block.

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:20.000
And so in the self-attention layer, every word, we calculate its contextual embedding

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:27.000
by weighting-- weighted averaging of its relationship to all other words in the sentence.

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:33.000
So the last word can see the first word, the first word can see the last word, and so on and so forth. But when we are doing next word prediction,

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:39.000
this feels problematic because you're peeking into the future.

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:45.000
So let's say that you want to predict the next word. If you look at this architecture, what it can simply

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:53.000
do, it can simply copy it from the input because it can see the whole sentence. So if I tell you, hey, the cat sat on the mat,

00:08:53.000 --> 00:09:01.000
if I just gave you, the cat sat on the, can you predict the next word for me? You'd be like, yeah, duh, it's mat.

00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:07.000
The whole thing becomes challenging only if I say, the cat sat on the dash. Now, predict the dash.

00:09:07.000 --> 00:09:13.000
So to put it another way, let's say that you want to predict-- you're fed in the first two words,

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:20.000
and you want to predict this. This is the right answer for the prediction. The network should only use the first two.

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:27.000
However, but because self-attention can see sat, it can see this next word, it will trivially

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:34.000
learn to predict the next word to be sat. There is no challenge for it.

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:41.000
So this is the key problem. This is the key problem with just using the transformer as is.

00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:48.000
AUDIENCE: What's our loss function here? RAMA RAMAKRISHNAN: The loss function in all these things is actually the same as before, which is that, for every output that's coming out--

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:53.000
so imagine you have just a traditional classification problem, in which you have one output,

00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:59.000
let's say, dividing-- you're classifying things to 10 categories, like we did with the Fashion MNIST, 10 digits.

00:09:59.000 --> 00:10:04.000
So you have 10 outputs, and that goes through a softmax. And then you have 10 probabilities.

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:12.000
And there we use cross-entropy. So here, for every one of these things, we use cross-entropy.

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:18.000
So we take this output, and there's a cross-entropy just for that, plus a cross-entropy for that, and so on and so forth.

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:23.000
So we minimize still cross-entropy, but the sum of all these cross-entropies. AUDIENCE: And does it get complicated

00:10:23.000 --> 00:10:28.000
at all by the fact that we have a large vocabulary size now, So lots of output? RAMA RAMAKRISHNAN: It gets complicated

00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:36.000
just because there are more things to worry about, compute and so on and so forth. But conceptually, no difference. Whether you have 10,000 or 50,000, it's the same thing.

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:41.000
It's just that, instead of classifying an input into one of 10 categories, the inputs themselves

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:49.000
are as long as the number of words in your sentence. So each word that comes into your sentence is being classified in one of 50,000 ways.

00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:54.000
So essentially, you have as many classification problems as you have number of words in a sentence.

00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:59.000
But at the end of the day, the loss function is the sum of all those things, or to be more precise, the average of all those things.

00:10:59.000 --> 00:11:06.000
Actually, I think I may have a slide about this, which I may have hidden because I wasn't sure

00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:17.000
if I would have time. Let's unhide it.

00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:25.000
And I did not agree ahead of time that we're going to set this up like this. All right, so, yeah, so we still use a classic--

00:11:25.000 --> 00:11:32.000
cross-entropy loss function. So each word that comes in, so the cross-entropy is actually

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:40.000
minus log probability of the right answer. And you may recall this from earlier in the class. So we just do the same thing for cat sat on the, everything,

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:47.000
and then we just take the average, 1 over 7. Boom, that's it.

00:11:47.000 --> 00:11:54.000
So to go back to this problem, so this is the issue. The issue is that we can't allow words to be

00:11:54.000 --> 00:12:01.000
predicted knowing the future. They should only know about the past words. So what do we do?

00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:08.000
We have to make a change to the transformer to make it work for next word prediction. So what we're going to do is when we are calculating

00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:14.000
the contextual embedding for a word, remember, the context embedding for a word is going to be a weighted average of all the other word's

00:12:14.000 --> 00:12:19.000
embeddings. We will simply give zero weight to future words.

00:12:19.000 --> 00:12:26.000
If you give zero weight to future words, it's almost as if they don't exist.

00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:32.000
And this will become clear in a second. So imagine that this is the thing we are going to calculate.

00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:39.000
These are all-- for every word in the sentence, we are calculating the pairwise attention weight.

00:12:39.000 --> 00:12:45.000
And you will remember, I went through this with an iPad thing last week. We calculate all the weights.

00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:52.000
So, for example, to find the-- so all these weights in every row will add up to 1.

00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:58.000
And so you take the contextual embeddings of the cat sat on the, multiply them by their respective weights that

00:12:58.000 --> 00:13:04.000
add up to 1, which is the first row of this table. And that gives you the contextual embedding for the word the, so on and so forth.

00:13:04.000 --> 00:13:09.000
And since we can't look at the future words, all we do is we go take this table

00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:17.000
and we just zero everything out in red. We just zero everything here out.

00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:25.000
And then we renormalize so that the remaining cells, the non-zero dot cells, will still add up to one in each row.

00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:30.000
So what that means is that, if you're actually only looking at the, only this thing is going to play a role.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:44.000
For cat, only this thing is going to play a role. So let's give an example. So to calculate-- to predict on, you'll only look at the words

00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:50.000
for the cat sat. The rest of it will not be considered at all.

00:13:50.000 --> 00:13:56.000
Now, the effect of doing all this is that-- by the way, this is called causal self-attention.

00:13:56.000 --> 00:14:01.000
This tweak is called causal self-attention. It's also called masked self-attention.

00:14:01.000 --> 00:14:08.000
Just different labels for the same thing. And so what that means is that, when you're looking at the input for the,

00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:15.000
only the is going to be used to predict cat. When you look, the cat, only these two

00:14:15.000 --> 00:14:24.000
are going to be used to predict sat and so on, and so on, and so forth.

00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:31.000
So this thing here, so all we do is we go into our transformer and we just change each attention head

00:14:31.000 --> 00:14:38.000
to be a causal attention head.

00:14:38.000 --> 00:14:43.000
And the way it's actually done under the hood is actually very elegant for computational efficiency

00:14:43.000 --> 00:14:52.000
purposes, but I won't get into it because it gets a bit involved. But the key idea is replace basic plain vanilla attention

00:14:52.000 --> 00:14:59.000
with causal attention, a.k.a., masked attention. When you do that, boom, suddenly, it

00:14:59.000 --> 00:15:07.000
starts working for an expert prediction. It can't cheat anymore. And when we do that, we get the transformer causal encoder.

00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:13.000
And, by the way, the word causal here, there's

00:15:13.000 --> 00:15:19.000
no connection to causality. So it's just a term.

00:15:19.000 --> 00:15:27.000
So if you look at the original transformer paper, it was created for translation, for machine translation,

00:15:27.000 --> 00:15:32.000
English to German, those kinds of use cases. So it had something called an encoder, which we are very

00:15:32.000 --> 00:15:39.000
familiar with from last week. And then it had something called a decoder. And it was called the encoder-decoder architecture.

00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:45.000
And we're not going to cover the encoder-decoder architecture because we are not covering machine translation in this class.

00:15:45.000 --> 00:15:50.000
But I'm mentioning this because this part of the architecture

00:15:50.000 --> 00:15:56.000
is called a decoder because it uses-- see, here there is a masked attention business

00:15:56.000 --> 00:16:05.000
going on here. Because it is using this masked attention, it's called decoder. So the transformer encoder is also

00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:11.000
referred to sometimes as the transformer decoder. But the word decoder has two meanings.

00:16:11.000 --> 00:16:17.000
It's a synonym for the causal encoder, like we have seen today. It's also used to refer to sequence-to-sequence translation

00:16:17.000 --> 00:16:23.000
problems for the second part of its architecture. So you just have to keep it-- it will become clear from context

00:16:23.000 --> 00:16:28.000
what we are talking about. In this course, of course, there is no confusion because we're not going to be looking at translation.

00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:34.000
We may say decoder, causal encoder. It's the same thing. AUDIENCE: So I thought there were some transformers that

