can we do with such a model? We have basically trained two different kinds of models, the one that can see everything, BERT, and the one that can't see the future, which is actually

00:20:26.000 --> 00:20:33.000
GPT. So what can you do with it? And we're going to come to that.

00:20:33.000 --> 00:20:43.000
All right, so now once you train such a model, given any input sentence, let's say that the sentence is, it was a dark and.

00:20:43.000 --> 00:20:48.000
It goes through all these things. And remember what I said earlier.

00:20:48.000 --> 00:20:55.000
The fact that it's predicting something after just seeing it, we don't really care.

00:20:55.000 --> 00:21:01.000
What we're really curious about is, what is the next thing it's going to say? And the next thing it's going to say

00:21:01.000 --> 00:21:06.000
is going to be, basically, what's coming out of this softmax.

00:21:06.000 --> 00:21:12.000
Does it make sense? We don't care about anything that went before it because we already

00:21:12.000 --> 00:21:18.000
have a half-formed sentence. And we want to just find the next thing here. So we only care about this.

00:21:18.000 --> 00:21:24.000
These things will come out of the architecture of the model, but we throw them out. We don't even pay any attention to them.

00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:31.000
We only look at what's coming out in this one here. And what comes out of that softmax, remember,

00:21:31.000 --> 00:21:36.000
is a 50,000 way table of probabilities. That's what a softmax is.

00:21:36.000 --> 00:21:45.000
It's a whole bunch of probabilities that add up to 1. And so it's going to-- and let's say, for example, that you have starting with aardvark all the way to zebra.

00:21:45.000 --> 00:21:52.000
And these are the probabilities. So it was a dark and, just for kicks,

00:21:52.000 --> 00:21:58.000
I put stormy as the most-- highest probability number. But these numbers will add up to 1.

00:21:58.000 --> 00:22:04.000
We have this table. And then what we do is we choose a token from this table.

00:22:04.000 --> 00:22:10.000
We get to choose. There's a whole bunch of numbers in this table, and we get to choose a token.

00:22:10.000 --> 00:22:16.000
The simplest thing one can think of is just choose the word that is the most likely, and we choose the word that's most likely here.

00:22:16.000 --> 00:22:23.000
And we're going to have a whole section on how to choose these things coming up. For now, let's go with the simple option.

00:22:23.000 --> 00:22:30.000
We're going to just choose the one that's most likely, 0.6. And then we attach it to the input.

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:36.000
So now the input has become, it was a dark and stormy. We run it through. And, again, we only care about the last one softmax.

00:22:36.000 --> 00:22:42.000
We do that, we get another table.

00:22:42.000 --> 00:22:47.000
And this table turns out-- the table keeps changing because the softmax is different for each time you run it through

00:22:47.000 --> 00:22:53.000
because the input has changed. So you get a new table, and it turns out the most likely one is night.

00:22:53.000 --> 00:23:02.000
And then we attached-- so night comes out the other end. And we attached night here, and we keep on going.

00:23:02.000 --> 00:23:09.000
We can keep on going maybe till we basically tell the model, OK, generate up to 100 tokens and stop.

00:23:09.000 --> 00:23:16.000
It might stop after 100. Or it might decide, the model may decide, in fact, that when it sees a punctuation, like a period, or an exclamation mark,

00:23:16.000 --> 00:23:22.000
or something, that's going to stop. And we have control over this when it stops and how it stops.

00:23:22.000 --> 00:23:29.000
But this is sort of the basic process. And you folks are all very used to it because you've all been playing with ChatGPT and the like.

00:23:29.000 --> 00:23:35.000
But the basic building block is next word prediction, feed it back to the input, next word prediction,

00:23:35.000 --> 00:23:41.000
keep on doing it. You keep on doing it, then, suddenly, it's writing entire novels for you.

00:23:41.000 --> 00:23:48.000
Yeah? AUDIENCE: The longer the initial input is, it's better. It's going to give you a better prediction?

00:23:48.000 --> 00:23:54.000
RAMA RAMAKRISHNAN: It depends on your objective. So, fundamentally, you have some task

00:23:54.000 --> 00:23:59.000
you want the thing to do for you. And that task may-- and you need to give it all the information

00:23:59.000 --> 00:24:07.000
it can possibly find useful. Yeah, so the long-- the more helpful the input, the better. Maybe that's how I would say it.

00:24:07.000 --> 00:24:14.000
Yeah? AUDIENCE: Would this also apply to something like Google Search or--

00:24:14.000 --> 00:24:20.000
because they also do next letter prediction too, but with this [INAUDIBLE]. RAMA RAMAKRISHNAN: Yeah, so the Google autocomplete,

00:24:20.000 --> 00:24:26.000
for example, I don't know if they actually use this kind of model under the hood or not. I just don't know.

00:24:26.000 --> 00:24:34.000
These things tend to be kept tightly under wraps. If they were to-- if they were using it, my guess is that they-- so I don't know if you folks have

00:24:34.000 --> 00:24:41.000
seen, recently, over the last few months, they have-- there is a generative AI panel that opens up when you do a Google search.

00:24:41.000 --> 00:24:46.000
That panel, I suspect, uses this, but I don't know if the default Google autocomplete actually

00:24:46.000 --> 00:24:53.000
uses it or not because it's very compute heavy. So I don't know what they do.

00:24:53.000 --> 00:24:59.000
So, yeah, this is what you do. Other questions on this, on the mechanics of it?

00:24:59.000 --> 00:25:05.000
Yeah? AUDIENCE: For our vocabulary list, I'm assuming it's static [INAUDIBLE].

00:25:05.000 --> 00:25:10.000
RAMA RAMAKRISHNAN: Yeah, correct. And as you will see here, it's not really a word vocabulary. It's a token vocabulary, but, yes, it

00:25:10.000 --> 00:25:18.000
is static for a given model. AUDIENCE: And I guess I'm assuming for Google or any other sort of search engine, that

00:25:18.000 --> 00:25:24.000
wouldn't necessarily be static. And so when it comes to, I guess, base--

00:25:24.000 --> 00:25:29.000
or even [INAUDIBLE] because the model would be different [INAUDIBLE].

00:25:29.000 --> 00:25:34.000
RAMA RAMAKRISHNAN: Sort of thinking about what happens to new words and things that are formed?

00:25:34.000 --> 00:25:45.000
And how does it handle it if the vocabulary is static? There's a very elegant solution that's coming up.

00:25:45.000 --> 00:25:51.000
All right, so now, in other words, we have learned how to do sequence generation.

00:25:51.000 --> 00:25:56.000
We already saw that we can do classification with BERT. We can do labeling with BERT, BERT-like models, which

00:25:56.000 --> 00:26:02.000
are trained on mass prediction. And for generating sequences, now we know how to do it. We just need to use a transformer causal encoder.

00:26:02.000 --> 00:26:13.000
Now, these kind of models, sequence generation models

00:26:13.000 --> 00:26:19.000
trained on text sequences using expert prediction, are called autoregressive language models or causal language models.

00:26:19.000 --> 00:26:26.000
And, of course, the GPT family is perhaps the most well-known example of an autoregressive language

00:26:26.000 --> 00:26:32.000
model, autoregressive because people who have done econometrics and some regression

00:26:32.000 --> 00:26:39.000
know the notion of autoregression means that you predict something, and then you use sort of the past predictions as inputs

00:26:39.000 --> 00:26:46.000
into the next time you predict. So this is the notion of autoregression. You predict, you feed the prediction back,

00:26:46.000 --> 00:26:51.000
get the next prediction, and keep on cycling through. Yes?

00:26:51.000 --> 00:26:57.000
AUDIENCE: So when you put it in as inputs into GPT, for example, and it has that--

00:26:57.000 --> 00:27:04.000
it shows you the next words as it's come in. Is that an indication of it doing this recalculation

00:27:04.000 --> 00:27:09.000
and what you described? RAMA RAMAKRISHNAN: Correct. That's exactly what's going on. In fact, if you use the API, that

00:27:09.000 --> 00:27:16.000
is the thing called the streaming API, where it will actually stream each token that's coming out through every pass.

00:27:16.000 --> 00:27:23.000
And you can actually see everything very clearly. But when you actually work with the web interface, and you see the thing almost as if it's typing,

00:27:23.000 --> 00:27:28.000
like a human, what I've heard from people-- I don't know if this is true. What I've heard from people is that they can actually

00:27:28.000 --> 00:27:34.000
do it much faster. They slow it down intentionally to give you the feeling that it's actually coming from a human.

00:27:34.000 --> 00:27:39.000
So it's like a UX trick to slow it down,

00:27:39.000 --> 00:27:45.000
to make it feel as if someone is actually typing something on the other end. So when you're interacting with a chatbot, for example,

00:27:45.000 --> 00:27:51.000
sometimes, you will see it actually typing. Slowly, you can see the bubble and you can see the typing. It's actually intentionally slowed

00:27:51.000 --> 00:28:00.000
down because you know it's a bot otherwise. So there's a little bit of UX creepiness, maybe, going on.

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:05.000
I don't know to what extent this is 100% true and how pervasive it is, but folks who work in the field

00:28:05.000 --> 00:28:11.000
have told me that this actually is not uncommon.

00:28:11.000 --> 00:28:17.000
So that's what's going on here. These are language models. And, of course, GPT-3 is an autoregressive language model.

00:28:17.000 --> 00:28:24.000
And the reason why we have an L in front of the LLM, because it was trained on lots of data with lots of parameters.

00:28:24.000 --> 00:28:30.000
At some point, it's not a small language model anymore. It's a large language model. Yeah, so it's LLM.

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:38.000
Nothing more momentous than that. So as it turns out, GPT-3 uses 96 transformer blocks,

00:28:38.000 --> 00:28:44.000
96 blocks,. And each block has 96 causal attention heads.

00:28:44.000 --> 00:28:51.000
And you can see-- you can read the GPT-3 paper. It gives you all the details of the architecture. That is interesting because, GPT-4, they didn't

00:28:51.000 --> 00:28:56.000
publish the architecture. From GPT-3-- after GPT-3, everything became closed.

00:28:56.000 --> 00:29:02.000
So we actually don't know what the architecture is, even though there's a lot of speculation on Twitter.

00:29:02.000 --> 00:29:08.000
But GPT-3, we know exactly what happened. It's 96 blocks. Each has 96 causal attention heads.

00:29:08.000 --> 00:29:13.000
And then the data was actually, they scraped $30 billion sentences from a whole bunch

00:29:13.000 --> 00:29:22.000
of sources web text, Wikipedia, a bunch of book databases. And then they basically just took those 30 billion sentences

00:29:22.000 --> 00:29:28.000
and just trained it exactly, next word prediction. That's it. Now, when they trained GPT-3, I think

00:29:28.000 --> 00:29:33.000
it cost them a lot of money because things were not as-- we

00:29:33.000 --> 00:29:40.000
hadn't figured out how to do it as efficiently as we know now, but it was still pretty amazing. And I'll talk about what is so special about GPT-3

00:29:40.000 --> 00:29:48.000
in just a minute or two. So this is what we have here. And as you folks have seen, the notion of generating text

00:29:48.000 --> 00:29:55.000
is very powerful because we can obviously generate text. But you can also generate code because code is just text.

00:29:55.000 --> 00:30:00.000
We can generate documentation for code. We can summarize text. We can answer questions. We can do chatbot. The list goes on.

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:08.000
All the excitement we see around GenAI from the time ChatGPT came out is precisely because this simple idea of text in,

00:30:08.000 --> 00:30:14.000
text out is just so flexible. It's so versatile. It can handle all sorts of use cases.

00:30:14.000 --> 00:30:20.000
That's why there's so much excitement. By the way, if you're really curious,

00:30:20.000 --> 00:30:26.000
I would actually recommend seeing this video, where this guy, Andrej Karpathy,

00:30:26.000 --> 00:30:31.000
builds GPT from scratch. It's a fantastic video.

00:30:31.000 --> 00:30:37.000
If you have even a little bit of curiosity about how these things are actually built, I would strongly recommend checking it out.

00:30:37.000 --> 00:30:44.000
And there's also a little blog post where this person-- basically, if you know NumPy, you can actually create GPT-3, GPT, using NumPy

00:30:44.000 --> 00:30:53.000
without using any frameworks and things like that. So I found it super interesting and helpful to understand

00:30:53.000 --> 00:30:59.000
what exactly is going on, so if you would like to do this. So now we're going to talk about decoding sampling

00:30:59.000 --> 00:31:07.000
strategies, which is, I said that when we produce-- when we come up with the softmax for that last token,

00:31:07.000 --> 00:31:14.000
we have 50,000 choices. What do we pick? As it turns out, to actually get really good performance out

00:31:14.000 --> 00:31:19.000
of GenAI systems, like ChatGPT, you need to be quite thoughtful about how to decode,

00:31:19.000 --> 00:31:28.000
how to actually sample from that table. So we'll talk about that for a bit. So first of all, definition, the process of choosing a token from

00:31:28.000 --> 00:31:33.000
the probability distribution from the-- coming out of the softmax-- I'm sticking this table right here.

00:31:33.000 --> 00:31:40.000
This is the softmax. This process of choosing it is called decoding. That's the technical term for it.

00:31:40.000 --> 00:31:46.000
We get this table, we have to decode, meaning we have to pick something from this table. That's called decoding.

00:31:46.000 --> 00:31:51.000
Now, there are two sort of extreme cases of very highly

00:31:51.000 --> 00:31:58.000
simple ways to do this. The first thing, of course, is just pick the one-- just pick the word with the highest probability.

00:31:58.000 --> 00:32:05.000
This is called greedy decoding. So in this case, for example, if stormy

00:32:05.000 --> 00:32:14.000
is 0.6, the highest probability in this whole table, we just pick stormy. So that is the obvious extreme simple case.

00:32:14.000 --> 00:32:22.000
The other thing we can do, which is also super simple, is that, because we have a probability table here, we can just reach into the table and sample a word out

00:32:22.000 --> 00:32:28.000
of it in proportion to its probability, which means that, if you have this table

00:32:28.000 --> 00:32:36.000
and you're sampling from it, if you sample from it 100 times, 60 times, you probably get stormy because the probability is 0.6.

00:32:36.000 --> 00:32:42.000
But some small fraction of the time, you may get strange things, like aardvark, and zebra, and so on and so forth.

00:32:42.000 --> 00:32:48.000
You're just literally doing random sampling. That's a fine way to do it too.

00:32:48.000 --> 00:32:56.000
Right. There's nothing wrong with that. So these are both options. So the key thing you need to remember is that--

00:32:56.000 --> 00:33:02.000
which one you pick. And there are some variations on it, which we'll get to in a moment. What you pick, which way to decode your pick,

00:33:02.000 --> 00:33:08.000
really depends on what your task is, what you're trying to use the system for, the LLM for.

00:33:08.000 --> 00:33:13.000
So the broad thing to remember is that, if you're working on questions

00:33:13.000 --> 00:33:21.000
for which the factual accuracy of the response is really important and/or you want the output to be

00:33:21.000 --> 00:33:28.000
deterministic, meaning every time you ask it a particular question, you really want the same answer back.

00:33:28.000 --> 00:33:33.000
You can imagine a customer call support agent, where the two different customers ask the same question,

00:33:33.000 --> 00:33:39.000
and they get different answers. You don't want that. So you want deterministic outputs.

00:33:39.000 --> 00:33:44.000
So in those situations, you should use greedy decoding. It's a good starting point because you will get--

00:33:44.000 --> 00:33:52.000
you won't get any random stuff because, for any given input sentence, the softmax that comes out of the table

00:33:52.000 --> 00:33:57.000
is not going to change. It's the same table. And if you're always picking the highest number in the table,

00:33:57.000 --> 00:34:03.000
that's not going to change either. So guaranteed determinism.

00:34:03.000 --> 00:34:08.000
And I've found that for reasoning questions and things where you're asking questions, math questions, reasoning

00:34:08.000 --> 00:34:15.000
questions, logic questions, you should really sort of keep it as greedy as possible in my experience.

00:34:15.000 --> 00:34:20.000
Now, there are other situations where random sampling is actually a better option.

00:34:20.000 --> 00:34:25.000
If you're doing creative things, write a poem, write a haiku, write a screenplay, things like that, you

00:34:25.000 --> 00:34:32.000
do want a lot of creativity, in which case, actually, randomness is your friend. You get a lot of different varieties of responses,

00:34:32.000 --> 00:34:38.000
diversity of responses. All that is really good. The price you pay for it is that, you lose determinism,

00:34:38.000 --> 00:34:44.000
the outputs are going to be stochastic. They're going to be random. They're going to vary from the same question. The answer is going to vary again and again.

00:34:44.000 --> 00:34:50.000
But in many cases, maybe it's OK. You don't care. So that's how, roughly, we think about it.

00:34:50.000 --> 00:34:57.000
The other one I want to say is that the diversity of response is also important because, if you imagine a chatbot, if you

00:34:57.000 --> 00:35:02.000
ask questions, if the chatbot always responds in the same stilted robotic fashion,

00:35:02.000 --> 00:35:09.000
it kind of starts to get annoying. You want some variation in the output because a human will never give you the same thing back.

00:35:09.000 --> 00:35:17.000
Though I must say that when I interact with call center agents, I think they're just cutting and pasting from a text library, so it does look kind of robotic.

00:35:17.000 --> 00:35:24.000
So maybe we are already kind of used to this. But anyway, so those are some of the things to keep in mind. Yeah?

00:35:24.000 --> 00:35:33.000
AUDIENCE: If you're using random sampling, do you end up with a better estimation of the uncertainty and probabilities that are more calibrated,

00:35:33.000 --> 00:35:39.000
in the sense that the table that you end up at the end is the real probability that you observe

00:35:39.000 --> 00:35:44.000
from the words in your corpus? RAMA RAMAKRISHNAN: The table doesn't change, regardless of how you sample it.

00:35:44.000 --> 00:35:51.000
The table is a starting point for sampling. All decoding is about what token from the table

00:35:51.000 --> 00:35:56.000
you're going to pull out. AUDIENCE: Oh, so it doesn't impact the loss function? RAMA RAMAKRISHNAN: No.

00:35:56.000 --> 00:36:02.000
Yeah, all those things are fixed. You literally get the table, and then you literally can forget how you got the table.

00:36:02.000 --> 00:36:09.000
And now decoding starts. AUDIENCE: Is that the reason why ChatGPT would generate

00:36:09.000 --> 00:36:15.000
a different answer given the same prompt if we run it again and again? Because they are using random sampling? RAMA RAMAKRISHNAN: Correct.

00:36:15.000 --> 00:36:22.000
That's exactly why. And we'll see-- I'll do a demo of it very shortly because you can actually manipulate it.

00:36:22.000 --> 00:36:28.000
AUDIENCE: If you do the prediction word-by-word, is there a way to make it resilient to mistakes?

00:36:28.000 --> 00:36:35.000
If you say, the night was dark and aardvark, that can mess up the next word, right? RAMA RAMAKRISHNAN: It can totally mess it up.

00:36:35.000 --> 00:36:40.000
AUDIENCE: So how does it get itself back on track? RAMA RAMAKRISHNAN: It cannot. And so great question.

00:36:40.000 --> 00:36:46.000
And we'll look at an example of things going off the rails in just a second. Yeah?

00:36:46.000 --> 00:36:51.000
AUDIENCE: Is this how Bing works, where you can slide between being more creative and more

00:36:51.000 --> 00:36:57.000
accurate? RAMA RAMAKRISHNAN: Yeah, exactly. So Bing has this creative, balanced, precise something. They're basically under the hood.

00:36:57.000 --> 00:37:03.000
They're manipulating some of the-- we're going to look at some of those parameters in just a moment. They're just manipulating it for you.

00:37:03.000 --> 00:37:10.000
But if you use the API, you can manipulate it directly.

00:37:10.000 --> 00:37:17.000
All right, so here is the basic thing to remember about random sampling.

00:37:17.000 --> 00:37:23.000
So our hope is that, for any given sentence, we think that there is probably some set

00:37:23.000 --> 00:37:29.000
of good answers for the next word and a whole bunch of bad answers, intuitively.

00:37:29.000 --> 00:37:34.000
So we want the probability of the good stuff.

00:37:34.000 --> 00:37:41.000
We want-- you can imagine a distribution as going like that. There is the head of the distribution, the first few words in the distribution, if you sort them

00:37:41.000 --> 00:37:49.000
from high to low probability. And then there is all the long tail of kind of inappropriate-- not inappropriate, irrelevant words.

00:37:49.000 --> 00:37:56.000
So our hope is that the model is so good that, for any given input phrase, it basically concentrates the output

00:37:56.000 --> 00:38:02.000
probability in the softmax, so just a few good words, and sort of, kind of zeroes out everything else.

00:38:02.000 --> 00:38:07.000
That is the ideal scenario because, in that scenario, if you do random sampling, by definition,

00:38:07.000 --> 00:38:16.000
you'll pick something from the high quality head of the distribution, and life is good. Now, we want random sampling to sample from the head

00:38:16.000 --> 00:38:26.000
and not from the tail. That's the key point. And what do I mean by head and tail? Let's be very clear.

00:38:26.000 --> 00:38:31.000
So imagine you have-- take the table that we looked at,

00:38:31.000 --> 00:38:38.000
the softmax table, which went from, what, about aardvark to zebra. And let's say we sort the table based on high to low probabilities.

00:38:38.000 --> 00:38:43.000
So maybe what's going to happen is that stormy is going to have a probability of, I don't know,

00:38:43.000 --> 00:38:52.000
0.6. And I think, if I remember right, a night had a probability of 0.3.

00:38:52.000 --> 00:39:00.000
And then there were a whole bunch of other words, all the way to the 50,000th word.

00:39:00.000 --> 00:39:07.000
From highest low probability, so this is what I'm-- so you can think of this as like a probability distribution

00:39:07.000 --> 00:39:13.000
And so, basically, what we are saying here is that this is the head of the distribution,

00:39:13.000 --> 00:39:20.000
while this long tail is the tail of the distribution. And we want our system to grab something from the head

00:39:20.000 --> 00:39:26.000
and not from the tail because the head is the stuff that's actually the relevant, useful, good stuff.

00:39:26.000 --> 00:39:33.000
That's really what we're trying to do here. Does it make sense? So to come back to this--

00:39:33.000 --> 00:39:39.000
and here is the most important point

00:39:39.000 --> 00:39:44.000
to remember about this slide. While the probability of choosing any individual word

00:39:44.000 --> 00:39:54.000
in this long tail is pretty small-- for any one word, it's pretty small-- the probability of choosing some word from the tail is high.

00:39:54.000 --> 00:40:01.000
So to go back to this thing here, yeah, so in this particular example, 0.6 plus 0.3,

00:40:01.000 --> 00:40:09.000
there is a 0.9 probability it's going to be either stormy or night. But there is a 10% probability it's going to be one of these words.

00:40:09.000 --> 00:40:21.000
And who knows what that word might-- it's going to be. It might be some random nonsense word. So what that means is then this goes to a point from before.

00:40:21.000 --> 00:40:27.000
If the LLM happens to sample a token from the tail, which is not good, it won't be able to recover from its mistake.

00:40:27.000 --> 00:40:33.000
It'll just go off the rails, which is why every word that gets generated, it's really important to get it right

00:40:33.000 --> 00:40:39.000
because it can't recover very often. AUDIENCE: Is there a technical way

00:40:39.000 --> 00:40:46.000
to define the difference between the head and the tail? RAMA RAMAKRISHNAN: No. [CHUCKLING] It's like this common thing people use.

00:40:46.000 --> 00:40:52.000
And the reason why it's not is because it's so problem dependent as to what the--

00:40:52.000 --> 00:40:59.000
