00:46:34.000 --> 00:46:41.000
All right, so to come back to this, what can we do? We can-- there are three ways in which you can tune random sampling to make it work for you.

00:46:41.000 --> 00:46:48.000
The first way and the idea of all these things is that you have some probability distribution.

00:46:48.000 --> 00:46:54.000
We are now going to sort of manually focus on the head. And then we're going to kill everything else,

00:46:54.000 --> 00:47:00.000
and only focus on the head and sample from that head, which immediately begs the question, how will you decide what the head is?

00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:07.000
And that was sort of Elena's question from before. How will you decide what the head is? So one way we do that is to say, you know what?

00:47:07.000 --> 00:47:13.000
I know we have 50,000 words in the vocabulary. I don't care. Each time, I'm only going to pick the top K words.

00:47:13.000 --> 00:47:18.000
K could be 10, 20, 30, 40, 50. That's very problem dependent. I'm going to pick the top 20 words,

00:47:18.000 --> 00:47:23.000
and I'm going to ignore everything else and only sample from the top 10 or the top 20. That's called top-k sampling.

00:47:23.000 --> 00:47:29.000
And so the way it works is that let's say this is your whole distribution. And I just stopped at red instead of going all the way

00:47:29.000 --> 00:47:34.000
to 50,000. And then you see and you decide, let's say that you want k to be 2.

00:47:34.000 --> 00:47:41.000
So you just grab the top two words, k equals 2. And then you renormalize the probability so they add up to 1.

00:47:41.000 --> 00:47:46.000
So 0.6 and 0.2, renormalize, it becomes 0.75 and 0.25.

00:47:46.000 --> 00:47:52.000
And now, just imagine that this is the new softmax table that you're sampling from. And you grab a number from--

00:47:52.000 --> 00:48:00.000
I'm sorry, a word from here and you're done. That's called top-k sampling, very commonly used.

00:48:00.000 --> 00:48:08.000
But it has a small shortcoming, which is that it basically assumes that this k that you've come up with, let's say 20,

00:48:08.000 --> 00:48:16.000
every input sentence, the right number of words in the head is 20, which seems-- obviously, it's not a well-supported assumption.

00:48:16.000 --> 00:48:22.000
It's just an assumption. So then the question becomes, can we do better? Because what you really want is you

00:48:22.000 --> 00:48:28.000
want the words that you pick to have the bulk of the probabilities, as much probability as possible.

00:48:28.000 --> 00:48:34.000
You don't really care how many words are inserted, as long as, together, they have a lot of probability, which brings us

00:48:34.000 --> 00:48:40.000
to something called top-p sampling, also called nucleus sampling, where instead of deciding on the number of words

00:48:40.000 --> 00:48:46.000
we're going to pick every time, we decide, you know what? We're just going to choose all the words, such

00:48:46.000 --> 00:48:52.000
that the probability of such words that we have chosen is at least p. Sometimes, it may be just two words.

00:48:52.000 --> 00:48:58.000
Sometimes, it may be 20 words. We don't care. And then we sample from it.

00:48:58.000 --> 00:49:03.000
So here, same thing here. Let's say you go with p equals 0.9.

00:49:03.000 --> 00:49:09.000
So 0.6 plus 0.2, 0.8 plus 0.1. 0.9. Boom, we have hit 0.9. We stop.

00:49:09.000 --> 00:49:16.000
And then we grab these three words, and then we renormalize them to get this thing. And then, boom, we sample from it.

00:49:16.000 --> 00:49:23.000
So this, actually, is even more effective in my opinion because it sort of fluctuates. It doesn't hardcode the number of words you think is important.

00:49:23.000 --> 00:49:29.000
Was there a question? Yeah. AUDIENCE: What if, let's say, 0.9 ended up--

