00:50:04.000 --> 00:50:11.000
I want to pick a word, which is the most likely word. You can do that. But if you say, I want a word--

00:50:11.000 --> 00:50:17.000
only consider words whose probabilities are at least something, then, basically, what you're saying is that I'm just going to keep on doing.

00:50:17.000 --> 00:50:26.000
And then we draw a line here. But the problem is, you don't know how many words have crept over your threshold.

00:50:26.000 --> 00:50:33.000
You might, for example, find that-- to go to your example, maybe-- you said 0.9 is the threshold. Maybe there are a whole bunch of-- there was a word at 0.89

00:50:33.000 --> 00:50:39.000
that you just missed because it didn't make the threshold. You'll be like oh no, I should have made it 0.89. So there's no right answer, unfortunately.

00:50:39.000 --> 00:50:46.000
But these are exactly-- this is exactly the kind of thinking that brought us these kinds of ways to tune these things.

00:50:46.000 --> 00:50:53.000
Sort of the foundation here is that-- the realization that we cannot sort of a priori decide what

00:50:53.000 --> 00:50:59.000
the right number of words is. So we have to find heuristics to try to do these things. So in practice, people try all these methods.

00:50:59.000 --> 00:51:04.000
In fact, you can do both. You can do-- you can set up so that you can do top-p and top-k at the same time.

00:51:04.000 --> 00:51:10.000
Basically, you're saying, grab words until you cross the probability or you

00:51:10.000 --> 00:51:19.000
cross k, whichever is earlier. So those are two methods people use heavily.

00:51:19.000 --> 00:51:26.000
The third method is called distribution. I'm sorry, temperature. And the idea of temperature is that, in top-k and top-p,

00:51:26.000 --> 00:51:32.000
it's sort of-- we have to decide on a number up front, k or p. And then we just draw the line and look at the words

00:51:32.000 --> 00:51:40.000
that pass the threshold. Temperature is like a softer way to do the same thing. It's a softer way to emphasize the head more than the tail.

00:51:40.000 --> 00:51:53.000
So I think iPad, all right.

00:51:53.000 --> 00:52:06.000
So the idea of temperature is, remember, when we have this, whoops, softmax, so aardvark all the way to zebra,

00:52:06.000 --> 00:52:13.000
you have all these probabilities. Now, remember, where did we get these probabilities? These probabilities came from a softmax.

00:52:13.000 --> 00:52:18.000
So what is a softmax? We basically had all these nodes,

00:52:18.000 --> 00:52:27.000
say 50,000 nodes in some output layer. And these were just numbers. Let's just call them a1 through a 50,000.

00:52:27.000 --> 00:52:36.000
And then we ran it through a softmax function. And what did it do? It basically did e raised to a1, e raised to a2, all the way to e

00:52:36.000 --> 00:52:42.000
raised to a, let's call it n. And then it divided it by the sum of all these things to get the probabilities.

00:52:42.000 --> 00:52:47.000
So this number became e raised to a1 divided by the sum of all

00:52:47.000 --> 00:52:54.000
the e raised to a's. So e raised to n divided by e raised to a1 plus e raised to a2

00:52:54.000 --> 00:53:00.000
and so on and so forth. So this is how softmax works. I'm just refreshing your memory from a few weeks ago.

00:53:00.000 --> 00:53:08.000
Now, what temperature does is that-- let me just write it a little easier.

00:53:08.000 --> 00:53:15.000
So e raised to a1 plus e raised to a2 plus all the way, n.

00:53:15.000 --> 00:53:24.000
What it does is it introduces a new parameter here called temperature, which is that we divide everything

00:53:24.000 --> 00:53:43.000
here by T. And the effect of adding this little knob

00:53:43.000 --> 00:53:49.000
called temperature here is very interesting. So let's assume for a second that T

00:53:49.000 --> 00:53:54.000
is a very, very small number. Assume that T is pretty close to 0, very small number.

00:53:54.000 --> 00:54:01.000
So if T is close to 0, what's going to happen

00:54:01.000 --> 00:54:07.000
is that, since it's in the denominator here, all these numbers, all these numbers

00:54:07.000 --> 00:54:13.000
are going to become really big because T is really small. If a1 happens to be a positive number,

00:54:13.000 --> 00:54:19.000
it's going to become really big. If a1 is a negative number, it's going to be a really, really small negative number. Now, in particular, what's going to happen

00:54:19.000 --> 00:54:26.000
is the biggest of all the a numbers, it was already big. Now it's going to get massive, which

00:54:26.000 --> 00:54:32.000
means that its probability is going to dominate everything else because you're taking a really big number and doing

00:54:32.000 --> 00:54:38.000
e raised to that number. So what's going to happen is that-- wait, what?

00:54:38.000 --> 00:54:56.000
is this? So if T is close to 0, the biggest a--

00:54:56.000 --> 00:55:06.000
hold on one second. The word corresponding to the biggest a

00:55:06.000 --> 00:55:12.000
will have a probability of 1 or close to 1.

00:55:12.000 --> 00:55:19.000
And since all the probabilities have to add up to 0, which means that everything else is going to be 0. So the biggest a will have a probability of 1.

00:55:19.000 --> 00:55:24.000
Everything else is going to have 0. So reducing temperature close to 0 means that the probability distribution

00:55:24.000 --> 00:55:29.000
is going to peak at the biggest word, and everything is going to become 0. So in practice, what that means is

00:55:29.000 --> 00:55:38.000
that if you look at something like this, if you apply temperature here, what's going to happen

00:55:38.000 --> 00:55:46.000
is that stormy's thing is going to get something like 0.999 and everything else is going to get wiped out.

00:55:46.000 --> 00:55:54.000
It's going to get really small, it's going to get even smaller, and so on and so forth.

00:55:54.000 --> 00:56:01.000
And so when T is exactly 0, basically, what that means is that this is going to be exactly 9-- 1, and everything is going to just get 0.

00:56:01.000 --> 00:56:10.000
So when one of them is 1 and everything else is 0, when you do sampling from it, you're just picking the big number, which means that it becomes greedy decoding.

00:56:10.000 --> 00:56:16.000
So that is the value of having temperature as a knob. Conversely, if you take temperature T

00:56:16.000 --> 00:56:22.000
and make it bigger and bigger, as opposed to smaller and smaller, this distribution is

00:56:22.000 --> 00:56:27.000
going to become flat, meaning all the words are going to have the same probability.

00:56:27.000 --> 00:56:34.000
So any one of these words becomes equally likely. So T close to 0, the biggest word gets picked.

00:56:34.000 --> 00:56:39.000
T close to, say-- exceeds 1, goes to 1.52, any word becomes likely.

00:56:39.000 --> 00:56:44.000
It becomes truly random. So that is the effect of temperature.

00:56:44.000 --> 00:56:50.000
And this knob, you can actually tune it.

